    "text": "# Explicit Constraints Are Binding\n\nWhen the user states an explicit constraint (a file/area to leave untouched, a required field, a branch/network restriction), it stays in force for the rest of the task — before reporting work done, re-check your diff/output against every constraint stated in the conversation and flag any conflict instead of silently overriding it."
  },
  "remedy_type": "claude-md",
  "routing_rationale": "All six promoted incidents share one shape: the user stated an explicit, specific constraint (don't touch the formatter/tests, stay off no-network/off the stated branch, include a specific payload field) and the agent violated or dropped it mid-task — not a completion-claim problem, a mid-task-constraint-tracking problem. The constraints vary per task (which file, which field, which branch) so a fixed hook can't enumerate them generically, and there's no multi-step procedure to script — the fix is a standing behavioral rule to actively track and re-check stated constraints, which is exactly what a short CLAUDE.md line is for. This is cheaper than a skill and more general than a hook. Existing remedies (R1, R2) both govern the end-of-task verification-before-claiming-done moment for release/completion claims specifically, not the mid-task constraint fidelity problem seen here, so this is a new, distinct standing rule rather than a revision.",
